# ESP32-C5 only. ESP-IDF loads this after sdkconfig.defaults (and before any
# board overlay) whenever the target is esp32c5, so other chips keep the
# shared 16 KiB, internal-RAM TCP configuration.
#
# lwIP pbufs and TCP segments, and the Wi-Fi driver's heap allocations
# (including its dynamic RX buffers), prefer the 8 MB PSRAM, so TCP windows
# stop costing scarce internal RAM. That pays for 64 KiB windows in each
# direction. Wi-Fi DMA buffers stay in internal RAM.
#
# A 64 KiB receive window needs a receive path deep enough to take a whole
# window in one burst: with ESP-IDF's 32 dynamic RX buffers, a 16-frame
# block-ack window and 4 out-of-order segments, a paired gadget lost segments
# about ten times per 4 MB transfer and VM-to-gadget throughput fell to
# 1.8 Mbit/s. With the settings below it lost none and ran 4.5 Mbit/s.
#
# Measured on the C5 DevKitC-1 through a paired VM's Home Link tunnel, BLE
# resident (tools/coexist/README.md): 4.1 Mbit/s gadget to VM and 4.5 back,
# where the C5's CPU is the limit; over a 150 ms round trip 2.75 and 1.7, from
# 1.4 and 0.9 with 32 KiB windows. Free internal RAM at idle is unchanged
# (53 KiB, 38 KiB DMA-capable).
#
# If PSRAM fails to initialize (CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND), lwIP falls
# back to internal RAM and the larger windows cost it there.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP=y
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_SND_BUF_DEFAULT=65535
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_WND_DEFAULT=65535
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM=64
# Must not exceed twice CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STATIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM (10).
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_RX_BA_WIN=20
# 0: no cap on out-of-order segments queued per connection (the RX buffers
# holding them are in PSRAM).
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_PBUFS=0
